d46b0cee48.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=134767739","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2023-07-22 09:41:13","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"<div class=\"fn\">\nElliott Howard Gilkey portrait</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"PD US|Files with no machine-readable author|Media contributed by the Ohio Digital Network|Media contributed by the Digital Public Library of America|Artworks without Wikidata item|Media contributed by Columbus Metropolitan Library|Institution templates with redirected Wikidata link|Digital Public Library of America files missing creator","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Artist":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page"},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<div class=\"description\">\nElliott H. Gilkey (2/8/1857-5/3/1948) was born in Warren, Ohio, the son of Sheldon Elliot and Emma (Roberts) Gilkey. In 1876, he came to Columbus when he was elected journal clerk of the Ohio Senate. He was employed by various businesses until 1888, when he was appointed chief bookkeeper in the office of the Auditor of State. In 1896, he returned to private business affairs until 1900 when he was elected as assistant clerk of the Ohio Senate. The following year, he was elected by the Ohio Supreme Court to be the first assistant law librarian. He then took over the position of law librarian in 1903 when the previous librarian died. Elliott married Florence Virginia Reed in 1884 and they were the parents of three children.</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"circa 1901<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P571,+1901-00-00T00:00:00Z/9,P1480,Q5727902</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"This file was contributed to Wikimedia Commons by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik